00:00:00.880 --> 00:00:02.550 align:start position:0%
 
hi there  day 85 you know i'm getting near the end  here i have to confess i didn't do much  today  uh i played golf with my wife  managed to get out of playing golf  tomorrow she's going with some friends i  managed to get out of that uh my  granddaughter's birthday this evening so  i didn't do much  uh i submitted some uh of my korean  writing for correction at our exchange  at link  and i got the corrections back i haven't  had a chance to to look at them so  tomorrow i've got to put some real  effort in i have a discussion lined up  with clara one of our tutors someone  asked me again how do i type korean on  the ipad so i can't really show that  because it the light shining on the ipad  i can't really show it but it's  extremely simple you can choose  different uh key keyboards for the ipad  as you can on a mac i don't know on  other computers so i can choose korean i  have a russian keyboard a czech spanish  chinese you name it  once you are in that language keyboard  then the little dictation icon  is linked to the language of the  keyboard  so then i can either type in korean and  i type by  pointing my finger at the keys on the  keyboard that are on the screen of the  ipad  or i can dictate and then it'll produce  the text more or less accurately  so i mean it's it's just very simple if  you have an ipad it's it's very handy  and as i said you just avoid the all the  mouse action so it's really quite  convenient um  and um yeah so i hope to do more writing  tomorrow i again thank my son for  you know basically  shaming me into starting to write i have  been writing more now and i feel more  confident when reading  so and i i know about as i've said  before i know that writing is important  i've just been too lazy to do it  um that's about it today was a very slow  day um  and quite honestly it's now 9 30. i'm  going to have a peek at  schuster live see what's happening there  uh after a while you know you see all  these politicians i must say the  politicians in the in ukraine i mean  they just  i mean  like this one fellow who was a candidate  for presidency he was in discussion with  some guy who was  involved in demonstrations in nikolai  effendi morales said you know you talk  pretty tough what if i show up and the  guy said well you just come so next  thing you know the guy's in nikolajf and  the next you know there's a fight going  there i mean the the degree of  sort of um  bravado  bully tactics  uh lack of compromise  exaggerating to make a point  it's amazing i don't see how they will  ever  achieve any compromise  that's just my impression  i mean it's fun to watch it's a sport  it's almost like a blood sport  but i mean obviously my hope is that  everything calms down and  and that they come to some kind of  solution  certainly  opinion polls even very recently don't  show a majority of people in the east  being in favor  of joining up with russia  but there are obviously some very active  people who represent a segment of the  population and who have some resources  whether they come from  uh yanukovych's party uh hangers on with  them or  oligarchs or whatever other sources uh  they're certainly keeping all that  action alive there  so we will just hope i mean really  it's not up to the americans the  europeans or the russians to tell the  ukrainians how to run their affairs  and uh hopefully uh  sane uh you know minds will prevail  the subject of language always comes up  and it's it's kind of presented  sometimes in next like you can't  forbid me from using my language well of  course no one is perverting the russian  speakers from using their language on  the other hand uh i'm curious about the  issue of uh the language of instruction  in universities so i actually sent an  email to two eastern ukrainian  universities one in donetsk and one in  kharkov and i said here i am  canada blah blah blah they have websites  in english and there are some leading  universities in the east and kharkiv and  elsewhere  and i can't remember which ones i sent  them to and i said can you tell me what  is the language of instruction of the  courses at your university  i haven't got an answer yet so i don't  know but it seems to me that that  if what the minister of education said  in that interview that i heard about a  month ago  that in the universities they only offer  courses in english and ukrainian then i  think that's a real problem on the other  hand i hear these complaints you know uh  are the the you know donetsk republic or  whatever they're saying you know we're  we're tired of uh our school books teach  us history that's anti-russian and that  glorifies  people like  bandera and stuff like that well i mean  yanukovych has been in power for the  last however many years so it's not you  can't tie that necessarily to the group  that's now in power in in kiev and who  are in any case an interim government so  i really think that all this talk about  referendum is extremely premature i  think there should be a presidential rec  uh election and then a parliamentary  election and then they deal with their  issues um  you know that but that's me sitting on  the sidelines i have no influence on  what goes on there and really it's up to  them to figure these things out for  themselves but  um i'm going off to listen to uh  schuster live and he normally has  two people from opposing camps so the  eastern ukrainian and the western  ukrainian they're going out at hammer  and tar and it's good for my russian and  uh good for my ukrainian which is  gradually improving anyway talk to you  later bye

